Every graded checkpoint is scored on an **analytic rubric**: a set of weighted criteria, each scored at one of four levels. The rubrics are published before you write, and they are the whole of the grade — there is no hidden component.

This page collects the grids themselves. The [syllabus]({{ CANVAS_BASE_URL }}/courses/{{ COURSE_ID }}/assignments/syllabus) explains how they add up.

---

## How to read a rubric

| Level | Points | What it means |
|---|---|---|
| **Exemplary** | 4 | Professional quality. Could serve as a model for the class. |
| **Proficient** | 3 | Solid and complete; meets the founder-grade bar. |
| **Developing** | 2 | Present but thin, uneven, or partly missing. Needs another pass. |
| **Beginning** | 1 | Missing, superficial, or seriously flawed. |
| *(Absent)* | 0 | Not submitted; no evidence. |

A criterion contributes **(level ÷ 4) × its weight**, and the weights sum to 100 within each assignment. So Proficient across the board is **75% — a C.** Aim for Exemplary; the grids below tell you exactly what that looks like on each criterion.

**Read the rubric before you start, not after you're graded.** Knowing what a criterion rewards is most of the work of satisfying it — and on a real project, finding out the evaluation criteria before you write is the move that separates people who get funded from people who are surprised.

> **Rubrics appear here as the term reaches them.** The grids below cover the checkpoints assigned so far. Later ones are published with their assignment and added to this page then.

